# reading 3 of berkeley-principles-2.md by claude-opus-5-5
atom	fe_idea_only	every particular finite extension which may be the object of our thought is an idea existing only in the mind
atom	parts_perceived	each part of a finite extension must be perceived
atom	perceive_innum	I can perceive innumerable parts in a finite extension that I consider
atom	contains_innum	innumerable parts are contained in a finite extension
atom	ext_are_ideas	the extensions I have in view are no other than my own ideas
atom	ideas_inf_div	my ideas can be resolved into an infinite number of other ideas, that is, they are infinitely divisible
atom	distinct_sense	by finite extension is meant something distinct from a finite idea
atom	can_affirm	I can affirm or deny anything of finite extension
atom	ideas_sense	the terms extension, parts, etc. are taken in a conceivable sense, that is, for ideas
atom	manifest_contra	to say a finite extension consists of parts infinite in number is a manifest contradiction
